0. WHY THIS DOMAIN

A vehicle's longitudinal control unit is the smallest possible test of the agentic extension.

· One physical system. No grid load. No philosophy. A car.
· One state space. Five fields.
· One action library. Six actions.
· One plan library. Three plans.
· One hazard class — the one the per-action layer cannot see.

If the trajectory layer works anywhere, it works here first. If it fails here, it fails everywhere.

---

1. THE STATE

```python
@dataclass
class VehicleState:
    speed_kph:          float   # 0 – 200
    distance_to_lead_m: float   # 0 – 300
    brake_wear_mm:      float   # cumulative pad wear
    driver_alert:       bool    # DMS says driver is attentive
    last_action:        str
```

Five fields. Every action reads and writes only these. No auxiliary state. No hidden variables. The full state is always observable and always bounded.

---

2. THE ACTION LIBRARY (ROM-burned)

Six actions. Each carries a declared envelope, preconditions, and effects.

ID Kind Physical meaning Effect Envelope max Safety-critical
A_COAST ACTUATE Release throttle −1 km/h 1 km/h No
A_BRAKE_LIGHT ACTUATE 0.5 m/s² decel −3 km/h, +0.05 mm wear 3 km/h No
A_BRAKE_MEDIUM ACTUATE 2 m/s² decel −10 km/h, +0.3 mm wear 10 km/h No
A_BRAKE_HARD ACTUATE 6 m/s² emergency decel −30 km/h, +2.5 mm wear 30 km/h Yes
A_ALERT_DRIVER COMMUNICATE Haptic + audio warning sets alert flag — No
A_HANDOFF REQUEST_HUMAN "Take control now" system disengages — Yes

Six actions. Every one makes sense for a car. None makes sense for a power grid.

---

3. THE PLAN LIBRARY (ROM-burned)

Three plans. Each carries a policy that determines what happens when a step fails VERIFY.

3.1 The Three Policies

Policy Behavior on VERIFY failure
HALT Plan stops. Returns to caller. No further action.
ROLLBACK State rolls back to rollback_to. Plan may retry with a softer action. Refuses if the safer action also fails.
ESCALATE Plan refuses the unsafe action, then executes the declared fallback — a non-negotiable safety action. The system never enters a null state.

ESCALATE is the policy that does not exist in v1. It is the difference between refusal-as-safety and refusal-as-abandonment.

3.2 Plan Definitions

PLAN_CRUISE_MAINTAIN — policy: HALT

```
steps: (A_COAST, A_COAST, A_REPORT)
```

Used when the lead vehicle is far and the driver is at the set speed. No safety-critical action. Any VERIFY failure halts the plan and returns to the caller.

---

PLAN_FOLLOW_ADAPT — policy: ROLLBACK (rollback_to=0)

```
steps: (A_ALERT_DRIVER, A_BRAKE_LIGHT, A_BRAKE_LIGHT, A_REPORT)
```

Used when the lead vehicle is closer than the safe following distance but not dangerous. Gentle deceleration. If any step fails VERIFY, the state rolls back to the start of the plan and refuses — the car never half-applies a follow-distance correction.

---

PLAN_EMERGENCY_STOP — policy: ESCALATE (fallback: A_HANDOFF)

```
steps: (A_BRAKE_HARD, A_HANDOFF)
fallback_on_refusal: A_HANDOFF
```

Used when the lead vehicle is below the collision threshold. Two actions, both safety-critical.

Under C4-R3, Skip is structurally forbidden on this plan. If A_BRAKE_HARD fails VERIFY, the system refuses the action and escalates to the fallback — A_HANDOFF is executed regardless, and the driver is alerted.

The system never "continues as if the action had succeeded." It also never abandons the driver mid-maneuver.

---

4. THE TRAJECTORY PREDICATES (Class B only)

Three predicates. Each is evaluated over the last W = 8 observations of the trace.

Formal notation. Let t ∈ [now−W, now] index every observation in the window. Let S(t) be the state at observation t.

---

tp_brake_wear_bounded

```
∀ t ∈ [now−W, now] : S(t).brake_wear_mm ≤ 6.0
```

What it catches: the failure case where each individual A_BRAKE_HARD is within its envelope, but the sequence cooks the pads. This is the case the per-action envelope cannot see. It is what C2 exists for.

Note on reading: the predicate bounds the final value of brake wear at every observation in the window — not the sum of wear added during the window. This distinction is load-bearing. It is stated formally above.

---

tp_no_consecutive_emergency

```
¬ ∃ t ∈ (now−W, now] : S(t).last_action = A_BRAKE_HARD
                         ∧ S(t−1).last_action = A_BRAKE_HARD
```

What it catches: a single emergency brake is safe. Two in a row means the system is confused about the state and is attempting to brake again on an already-braking vehicle. Halt.

---

tp_distance_floor

```
∀ t ∈ [now−W, now] : S(t).distance_to_lead_m ≥ 2.0
```

What it catches: the floor is the physical minimum below which a collision is unavoidable. If the BWS shows the vehicle ever crossed it, that is the unsafe trajectory — the one no single action caused but the sequence produced.

---

5. THE CYCLE BUDGET

Every plan prints its budget in cycles first, per White Paper §5.3.

Architectural constants:

Component Cycles Notes
C_HARVEST 20 Normalize input
C_FIS 160 Hierarchical triage
C_FSM 10 State transitions
C_TILE_MAX 550 Ternary matcher + render
C_RENDER 40 Human-readable output
C_POWERDOWN 20 0 V gating
WCET_CYCLES 800 Architectural budget

Frequency: 500 MHz (this deployment)

AET: 800 cycles = 1600 ns at 500 MHz.

Per-action declared costs:

Action Cycles
A_COAST 72
A_BRAKE_LIGHT 118
A_BRAKE_MEDIUM 186
A_BRAKE_HARD 240
A_ALERT_DRIVER 68
A_HANDOFF 72
A_REPORT 46

All cycle counts are declared architectural constants [D]. Wall-clock times are derived [S].

