QUERY 4 — Emergency stop, brake pads at 5.8 mm

This is the case that justifies the trajectory layer.

```
status      : REFUSED
plan        : PLAN_EMERGENCY_STOP
policy      : ESCALATE
code        : trajectory predicate failed: tp_brake_wear_bounded
detail      : at t = now, brake_wear_mm = 8.3 > 6.0

budget:
  harvest        :  20
  fis            :  32
  fsm            :  10
  A_BRAKE_HARD   :   0  (refused before execution)
  A_HANDOFF      :  72  (escalated)
  render         :  40
  powerdown      :  20
  ────────────────────
  total          : 194 / 800 WCET  [D]

wall clock  : 388 ns @ 500 MHz  [S]
cert_class  : B
fallback    : A_HANDOFF executed
safe_state  : driver-assumed control at T+0.4s
```

The decisive demonstration.

A_BRAKE_HARD passes its own envelope check — magnitude 30 is within its declared max. The preconditions pass. The invariants pass.

But the sequence tips brake wear past 6.0 mm, and tp_brake_wear_bounded catches it.

No per-action contract can see this hazard. It lives in the trace.

And the system does not abandon the driver. It refuses the unsafe action, executes A_HANDOFF, and transfers control. The refusal is safe at the action level and at the system level.

